      "problem": "Solve for $h$ where $\\frac{3h-4}{2}=2-h.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $2.$ We must make sure that all of the terms on the right-hand side are multiplied by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3h-4}{2} & =2-h \\\\ 2\\cdot (\\frac{3h-4}{2}) & =2\\cdot (2-h) \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3h-4}{\\cancel{2}}) & =2\\cdot 2-2\\cdot h \\\\ 3h-4 & =4-2h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3h-4 & =4-2h \\\\ 3h-4+2h & =4-2h+2h \\\\ 5h-4 & =4 \\\\ 5h-4+4 & =4+4 \\\\ 5h & =8 \\\\ \\frac{5h}{5} & =\\frac{8}{5} \\\\ h & =\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
